fix(index): turning on the drive index for a server can no longer start a local walker on a scheme root

`local_external::classify` read only two facts, a live smb2 session and the mount's network flag, and neither catches a
server. An SFTP volume's root is `sftp://ada@nas:22/srv`, which is not a mount point, so `detect_filesystem_for_path`
reports it as NON-network and the volume sailed through as a plain local external drive. `enable_drive_index` takes any
volume id and only special-cases root and MTP, so the whole thing was reachable over IPC: `start_volume` would spin up
the local guarded walker plus an FSEvents watcher rooted at a scheme path, after persisting the sticky "the user turned
this on" marker. The walk covers zero entries and the drive is then reported as indexed, so folder sizes and search
answer "nothing here" for a server full of files. The frontend's capability row was the only thing keeping a human away
from it, and the badge half of that landed in `51ca90bb6`.

`routes_to_local_external` now takes the volume's `BackendKind` instead of an is-SMB boolean, and only `Local` may run
the local walker. Both halves of the gate stay load-bearing and neither subsumes the other: a network mount is served by
a `LocalPosixVolume` and so answers `Local`, while a server's scheme root probes as non-network. `smb/index.rs` already
had the right shape (`!matches!(backend_kind(), Smb | Local)` → refuse); this is its twin.

The `cover/bootstrap.rs` caller passes the kind through too, so the one rule serves both sites. It was already safe
there behind `supports_local_fs_access()`, and now it doesn't depend on that ordering.

Cells: `classify` falling through for `Sftp`, `Webdav`, and `Adb` over a mount that probes clean, plus the pure
predicate refusing all six non-`Local` backends.
